#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573} :activity/id #uuid "210826c6-78d3-519b-84ab-0d8eb2721083" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766 :topic/id #uuid "64ed08fd-258d-5456-ab85-4c3d693a62d2" :topic/math-academy-id 3687} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180764 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll discuss the concepts of **work** and **kinetic energy** and describe how these concepts are related. \n\nThe **work done by the force** $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from the position $x=x_1$ to the position $x=x_2$ along the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\nW = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} F(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons ($\\textrm{N}$) and meters ($\\textrm{m}$), respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$).\n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ is moving along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $4x^3\\,\\textrm{N},$ acting in the direction of increasing $x,$ as shown below. Distances along the $x$-axis are measured in meters.\n\n![](images/3f/3f0040d7a71563304cb954908988c82df1774a1197a5ce16c74a3b8ac65e04a0.png)\n\n \n\nLet's calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=2.$\n\nSubstituting the given values into our formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =16-1 \\\\  & =15\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the work done by $F$ in moving $P$ from $x_1=1\\,\\textrm m$ to $x_2=2\\,\\textrm m$ equals $15\\,\\textrm J.$\n\nWe can use the concept of work in conjunction with Newton's second law. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "33cf6fa3-5f8d-579b-990b-ddeb9d1e3b8a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7799 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "fa2ce148-bfaa-55f3-a775-56631c5bd980" :step/math-academy-id 45122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179777 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179438 :question/id #uuid "622bd0ff-d08a-5504-8b38-8bc388bfcbf5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12525"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9696b3f4-42c8-5796-be3a-6c59c9600c95" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050723}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180099} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284403} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284405} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427393}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Work Done by a Force"} :step/id #uuid "cb90c47f-c22d-5b7f-a72a-cfc50bb4fdcd" :step/math-academy-id 45123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179778 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179439 :question/id #uuid "4e428ee4-0017-5e83-887c-26e723b4af08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12526"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec390427-7510-57ea-aedf-a930284f93b5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049947}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180101} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416270}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Kinetic Energy of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "54dddd69-accb-5eb8-beac-e9f9dbc87bf7" :step/math-academy-id 45124 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179779 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179440 :question/id #uuid "39eab788-1a8d-51af-b535-594cacc8ec2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94904fa2-bf02-5486-950c-979a4d12998e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284401} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416268}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle To Find the Work Done by a Force"} :step/id #uuid "496c1449-0914-5643-b8b4-95ec09fb5867" :step/math-academy-id 45125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179441 :question/id #uuid "8f79d7e9-377f-5012-a762-279839fc806a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "793154ca-29f0-5552-97cd-57acbc915966" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284404} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416269}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Work-Energy Principle to Find a Particle's Velocity"} :step/id #uuid "b02a7662-acf4-57da-98d7-bae7093076f2" :step/math-academy-id 45126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180765 :tutorial/content "The **kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv^2.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms ($\\rm{kg}$) and meters per second ($\\rm{m/s}$), respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($\\rm{J}$). \n\nFor example, suppose a particle $P$ of mass $5\\,\\rm{kg}$ is moving along the $x$-axis from rest at the origin with an acceleration of $3t^2\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ where $t \\geq 0$ is the time in seconds. Let's calculate the particle's kinetic energy at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}.$\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0 = 0^3 + C\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nC = 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t) = t^3.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,\\rm{s}$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2) = 2^3 = 8\\,\\rm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =160\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Kinetic energy is a *scalar* quantity.\n\n- You probably noticed that kinetic energy and work have the same units. This is not a coincidence! We'll learn more about that shortly.\n\n- In practical applications, it's often more important to calculate a *change* in the kinetic energy of a particle between two points in time. \n\nThe **change in kinetic energy** of a particle with mass $m,$ **initial velocity** $v_0,$ and **final velocity** $v_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta \\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12mv_1^2 - \\dfrac12mv_0^2.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42f83ae3-3805-546b-a5bf-264b8b56d6ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7811 :tutorial/title "Kinetic Energy"} :step/id #uuid "3bec1351-7ee4-5f29-8760-5dd679edda2e" :step/math-academy-id 45179 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180766 :tutorial/content "The **work-energy principle** states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW = \\Delta\\, \\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta\\, \\text{KE} = \\dfrac12 m v_1^2 - \\dfrac12 m v_0^2\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, $v_0$ is its initial velocity, and $v_1$ is its final velocity." :tutorial/id #uuid "e00f462b-c087-5c42-afc7-bfeffad5906b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7812 :tutorial/title "The Work-Energy Principle"} :step/id #uuid "4d3d3f00-8ddb-5882-984a-2f48d2b19da6" :step/math-academy-id 45180 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180576}}] :activity/title "The Work-Energy Principle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179842}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427396} :activity/id #uuid "595c8569-5220-5289-addf-624ae85604ef" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050766 :topic/id #uuid "64ed08fd-258d-5456-ab85-4c3d693a62d2" :topic/math-academy-id 3687} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427394 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb4d826c-14ba-585c-aebd-1facb3368012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab762ed-0112-5d52-b62f-05b3b484b0b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389452} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa053a0-465d-5f0b-8742-c3a588a86e3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389453} {:answer/id #uuid "ee6add72-368a-56d1-a701-8e8fbe0ceee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389454} {:answer/id #uuid "04400f5c-b01f-5a74-aa41-074c903d6463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389455}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389451} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fc72668-2f1d-508f-943f-15caead3623c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d31a00a5-c404-50d8-b7f2-fda34cf2cc53" :question/math-academy-id "q-163685" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=1$ to $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{4}-1) \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "593e6b92-f812-5412-8a5a-9a03f302bd99"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427395 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427393 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005f40cc-1636-56ab-b246-5737a11e79cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427387} {:answer/id #uuid "49730251-5fc6-518d-bdf0-26723df97b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427388} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6cfef4-3fa0-536d-accc-16bd9fddb64c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427389} {:answer/id #uuid "a1bab12d-028f-5c7b-8260-e0dd0f56b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427390} {:answer/id #uuid "ce34ff78-907d-5c9a-b016-26b849102736" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427391}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427391} :answer-field/id #uuid "7df70823-9962-541f-9d3b-9f6bc58730dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "720a885d-9cf4-5861-8b99-013e8190ec62" :question/math-academy-id "q-163683" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a force of magnitude $(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{N}$ acting in the direction of increasing $x.$ Given that $x$ is measured in meters, calculate the work done by the force in moving the particle from $x=0$ to $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The work done by a force $F=F(x)$ in moving a particle from $x={x}_{1}$ to $x={x}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nW={\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}F(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhen the force and distance are measured in Newtons and meters, respectively, the work done by the force is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nSubstituting the given values into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3})-({0}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}({0}^{3})) \\\\  & =4+\\frac{8}{3}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{3}\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b7802f0-312d-58fa-a3d7-a69103bfe77b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427394}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427396 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452dcb36-4e8e-5c4d-bbab-d59aa1427bbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279210} {:answer/id #uuid "675b5d28-9c02-5ed8-b190-60068bfd7200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279529} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcd8992-c86f-5ed8-8b85-3b006eb23206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279696} {:answer/id #uuid "98b9878b-6e3c-5263-8d0a-a4133c54e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279941} {:answer/id #uuid "f3589965-bf1c-56f3-9f99-d2824d583527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10\\,J" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279941} :answer-field/id #uuid "3768d6e3-78a3-5ed2-b58b-bc35eeb1c01e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c92a85dc-fea5-582a-a4d0-c749606f94b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163601" :question/problem "A particle $P$ of mass $2\\,kg$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a resultant force. The particle's velocity is $2t\\,m/s,$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the work done by the force between the times $t=1\\,s$ and $t=2\\,s.$" :question/worked-solution "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=Δ\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\nΔ\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=2t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=1$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(1)=2(1)=2\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(2)=2(2)=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =Δ\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (4)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot (2)^{2} \\\\  & =16-4 \\\\  & =12\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fa49f09b-6aed-5e72-bb78-032089ad9e8e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427395}}] :activity/title "The Work-Energy Principle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186427386}]}
